    "body": "  ## Motivation\r\n  `_compute_pid` in `python/sglang/srt/batch_invariant_ops/batch_invariant_ops.py` accepts a `NUM_SMS` argument but never uses it — the tile → `(pid_m, pid_n)` mapping depends only on `tile_id`, `num_pid_in_group`, `num_pid_m`, and `GROUP_SIZE_M`.\r\n\r\n  Dropping the unused parameter makes it explicit that per-tile assignment is  independent of the SM count, which is one of the properties the batch-invariant kernels rely on (the caller's persistent grid loop uses `NUM_SMS` as its stride, but the mapping itself does not).\r\n\r\n  ## Modifications\r\n  - Remove `NUM_SMS` from the `_compute_pid` signature and from its two call sites (`matmul_kernel_persistent`, the persistent bmm kernel).\r\n  - No behavior change: the helper is inlined by Triton and the removed argument was dead. Verified on H100 that outputs are bit-identical before/after.\r\n\r\n  ## Checklist\r\n  - [x] Format your code / pre-commit\r\n  - [x] No functional change intended\n\n<!-- pr-states:start -->\n---\n### CI States\n\nLatest PR Test (Base): <!-- slot:pr-test:start -->:x: [Run #30697708892](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/30697708892)<!-- slot:pr-test:end -->\nLatest PR Test (Extra): <!-- slot:pr-test-extra:start -->:x: [Run #30697708853](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/30697708853)<!-- slot:pr-test-extra:end -->\n<!-- pr-states:end -->",
